Q: Is there a concept in Python 3 which integrates with the while-statement like an iterator does with the for-statement? A: <p>I don't think the OP is unaware of how a <code>for</code> loop can be implemented with a <code>while</code> loop. The question is about how the specific code pattern can be structured in a way that is both readable and easy to use (i.e. without an explicit, separate initialization in the user's code).</p>
<p>By the way, there is no need to use a flag in your sample implementation of a <code>for</code> loop. A <code>break</code> would be enough:</p>
<pre><code>_some_iterator = iter([1, 2, 3])
while True:
    try:
        x = next(_some_iterator)
    except StopIteration:
        break
    print(x)
</code></pre>

Q: Iteratively get a mut reference from a nested structure A: <h2>Why your code doesn't work</h2>
<p>Here's a very cut-down version of your code, which doesn't do anything useful (this version of <code>List</code> is so cut-down that it can't even be constructed) but which is much shorter and will help to make the problem clearer (<code>List</code> corresponds to <code>Node&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>&amp;'a mut &amp;'b mut List</code> corresponds to <code>&amp;'a mut Getter&lt;'b, T&gt;</code>):</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>struct List {
    next: Box&lt;List&gt;,
}

fn tail&lt;'a, 'b&gt;(s: &amp;'a mut &amp;'b mut List) {
    *s = &amp;mut *s.next;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This fails to compile with &quot;error: lifetime may not live long enough&quot; and &quot;assignment requires that <code>'a</code> must outlive <code>'b</code>&quot;.</p>
<hr />
<p>A somewhat surprising fact of Rust's borrow checker is that from an <code>&amp;'a &amp;'b T</code> it is possible to produce an <code>&amp;'b T</code> (i.e. the inside, longer lifetime), but from an <code>&amp;'a &amp;'b mut T</code> it is only possible to produce an <code>&amp;'a T</code> (i.e. the outside, shorter lifetime).</p>
<p>The reason is that because an <code>&amp;'a &amp;'b T</code> can observe an <code>&amp;'b T</code>, that acts as a proof that it is safe to shared-borrow a <code>T</code> for the entire lifetime <code>'b</code> – the <code>&amp;'b T</code> proves that the content of the <code>T</code> will not change for the entire lifetime <code>'b</code>. However, an <code>&amp;'a &amp;'b mut T</code> does not observe anything that proves that that the memory will not change – once the lifetime <code>'a</code> is over, the <code>&amp;'b mut T</code> could be used to change the referenced memory and that would break the invariants of any <code>&amp;'b T</code> references that existed at the time.</p>
<p>In other words, when you reborrow the inside of a nested reference, you get the longer lifetime when the inner reference is shared – but when the inner reference is mutable, you only get the shorter lifetime.</p>
<hr />
<p>In the program I wrote above, what is happening is that after I take <code>&amp;mut *s.next</code>, the resulting reference has the shorter lifetime <code>'a</code>. But <code>*s</code> is supposed to contain references with the longer lifetime <code>'b</code>, and so the assignment is not valid.</p>
<p>You might be wondering whether this is just a limitation of the Rust borrow checker, or whether there's an actual potential unsoundness. As it turns out, there &quot;sort of&quot; is an unsoundness here. Rust's borrow checker is entirely based on the signature of the methods and functions that are called, and does not look at their actual implementations: and if some of the methods used had different behaviour (but behaviour that would be legal based on their declared type), this could be unsound.</p>
<p>Imagine what would happen if someone wrote a malicious implementation of <code>&lt;&amp;'a mut &amp;'b mut List as DerefMut&gt;::deref_mut()</code> – this hypothetical malicious dereferencing implementation pretends to deference the reference, but it actually returns a <em>different</em> piece of memory (containing a valid <code>&amp;'b mut List</code> value) each time it's called. (This behaviour would be weird, but compatible with <code>deref_mut</code>'s type signature.) Taking <code>&amp;mut *s.next</code> produces a reference into the interior of the first piece of memory returned. Assigning it to <code>*s</code> then writes that reference into a <em>different</em> piece of memory. There are now two copies of the mutable reference: throughout the rest of lifetime <code>'a</code> this isn't a problem because the original copy is reborrowed-from and can't be used, but once <code>'a</code> ends, there are two usable copies of the reference (in the first-returned piece of memory and in the secon-returned piece of memory), both of which claim to be valid for lifetime <code>'b</code>, and so during the rest of <code>'b</code> (outside <code>'a</code>) there are two aliasing mutable references, neither of which is currently reborrowed, and that breaks Rust's aliasing assumptions. As such, the borrow checker only allows this if <code>'a</code> and <code>'b</code> are the same lifetime (because the problem only occurs during the time inside <code>'b</code> but outside <code>'a</code>).</p>
<p>Of course, there's very little chance that <code>DerefMut</code> on mutable references will ever be redefined, especially to do something as unintuitive as this. But the borrow checker doesn't care (and probably can't be made to care without making its performance too bad to be useful) – its reasoning is entirely type-based, and looking only at the types doesn't give enough information to be able to prove this code sound.</p>
<h2>What to write instead</h2>
<p>In order to make the code work, the inner reference needs to be moved out of the outer reference rather than reborrowed from it. That generally requires adding a placeholder possibility for the inner reference, so that the outer reference has something valid to point to while the inner reference isn't there.</p>
<p>My example can be made to work like this (which compiles successfully):</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>struct List {
    next: Box&lt;List&gt;,
}

fn tail&lt;'a, 'b&gt;(s: &amp;'a mut Option&lt;&amp;'b mut List&gt;) {
    let t = s.take().unwrap();
    *s = Some(&amp;mut *t.next);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Instead of reborrowing from <code>*s</code>, the program now temporarily moves out of <code>*s</code>: this way, there are never two copies of any of the mutable references, no matter how malicious their <code>DerefMut</code> implementations are. <code>*s</code> is made an <code>Option</code> so that it can temporarily store <code>None</code> while its content is being replaced by its <code>next</code>, and stores <code>Some</code> the rest of the time. (In a real program, instead of using <code>unwrap</code>, you would need to write code to handle the case where the <code>Option</code> target were missing.)</p>
<p>The same technique could be used with your program: <code>Getter</code> should store an <code>Option&lt;&amp;'a mut Node&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> rather than an <code>&amp;'a mut Node&lt;T&gt;</code>, and then <code>get</code> should move out of <code>self.0</code> (and move back in) rather than reborrowing. One big disadvantage of this technique is that it produces an invalid-but-representable <code>Getter</code> value <code>Getter(None)</code> which you will have to deal with everywhere in your program (potentially by panicking, if you can't find a better use for it). But at present, it's the best that Rust can do in safe code. I consider this to be a considerable shortcoming of Rust – it comes up very frequently and makes the code less maintainable and sometimes less performant – but I'm not aware of any current ideas on how to fix it.</p>
<p>(In order to avoid the performance loss, you can do essentially the same thing in <code>unsafe</code> code by using <code>MaybeUninit</code> rather than <code>Option</code> and tracking the <code>Some</code>/<code>None</code> state manually. I wouldn't recommend this unless the code becomes a major bottleneck, though, because it is difficult and annoying to prove, and you run the risk of undefined behavior if you get it wrong.)</p>

Q: MongoDb Connection failed : Error: querySrv ECONNREFUSED A: <p>Now I've found the root cause. This is not a problem with MongoDB. This is a problem with Node.js. When I chose Node.js <code>18.20.8</code> version, it worked perfectly.</p>
<hr />
<p>Or you can use the latest version of Node.js like <code>(20 , 25)</code></p>
<pre><code>import dns from &quot;node:dns/promises&quot;;

dns.setServers([&quot;1.1.1.1&quot;, &quot;1.0.0.1&quot;]);
</code></pre>

Q: Test queue job withoutOverlapping with dontRegister method A: <p><strong>1. Use ShouldBeUniqueUntilProcessing</strong></p>
<p>This prevents duplicate jobs from being queued for the same model.</p>
<pre><code>use Illuminate\Contracts\Queue\ShouldBeUniqueUntilProcessing;
use Illuminate\Contracts\Queue\ShouldQueue;
class InspectModelJob implements ShouldQueue, ShouldBeUniqueUntilProcessing
{
    public function __construct(public int $modelId) {}

    public function uniqueId(): string
    {
        return &quot;inspect-model-{$this-&gt;modelId}&quot;;
    }

    public function handle()
    {
        // heavy validation logic
        // broadcast progress / result
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Result</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>If inspection is running → new dispatch is ignored</p>
</li>
<li><p>No queue pile-up</p>
</li>
<li><p>No manual locks needed</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>2. Dispatch normally (Controller / Service)</strong></p>
<pre><code>InspectModelJob::dispatch($model-&gt;id);
</code></pre>
<p>Laravel handles the uniqueness for you.</p>
<p><strong>3.Unit Test:</strong></p>
<p>Test: Job is dispatched only once</p>
<pre><code>use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Queue;
public function test_model_inspection_job_is_not_dispatched_twice()
{
    Queue::fake();

    InspectModelJob::dispatch(1);
    InspectModelJob::dispatch(1);

    Queue::assertPushed(InspectModelJob::class, 1);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This validates no overlapping jobs are queued</p>
